


NEWFS(8)	    UNIX Programmer's Manual		 NEWFS(8)



NAME
     newfs - construct a new file system

SYNOPSIS
     /etc/newfs [ -N ] [ -m free-gap ] [ -b ] [ -B bootblock ]
     special disk-type

DESCRIPTION
     _N_e_w_f_s is a ``friendly'' front-end to the _m_k_f_s(8) program.
     _N_e_w_f_s will look up the type of disk a file system is being
     created on in the disk description file /_e_t_c/_d_i_s_k_t_a_b, calcu-
     late the appropriate parameters to use in calling _m_k_f_s, then
     build the file system by forking _m_k_f_s.

     The -_N option causes the file system parameters to be
     printed out without actually creating the file system.

     The -_m option allows the specification of the block inter-
     leaving of the free list (the _m parameter of _m_k_f_s).  If not
     specified or outside the range 0 thru 31 then _m is given a
     value of 2.  The _n parameter to _m_k_f_s is calcuated by _n_e_w_f_s
     to be 1 cylinder in size.

     The -_b and -_B options specify that a primary block zero
     bootstrap should be installed in block zero of _s_p_e_c_i_a_l.
     This is typically done for `a' partitions to make disks
     bootable.	If -_b is specified, _n_e_w_f_s will attempt to use a
     default boot block (defined in /_e_t_c/_d_i_s_k_t_a_b).

     The -_B option allows one to specify the file to read the
     boot block from (_b_o_o_t_b_l_o_c_k).  Note that a boot block can
     always be copied separately via:

	  dd if=_b_o_o_t_b_l_o_c_k of=_s_p_e_c_i_a_l bs=512 count=1 conv=sync

FILES
     /etc/disktab   for disk geometry and file system partition
     information
     /etc/mkfs to actually build the file system
     /mdec/*   block zero bootstraps

SEE ALSO
     disktab(5), fs(5), diskpart(8), fsck(8), format(8), mkfs(8)

